    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1689">

                <pb facs="1333" n="1323" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg1323"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">702.4193/10–1949: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Strong</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">at Chungking</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-10-09T18:00:00-05:00">October 19, 1949—6
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>98. Brit FonOff has asked Brit Emb Nanking ensure appropriate arrangements made
                    for assumption US interests in cases where Brit US consular districts do not
                    coincide. To assist this respect forward Chungking list Amcits Kweichow (Deptel
                    91 Sept 28 para A<anchor n="65" xml:id="d1689-f1"/>). Discuss <pb facs="1334"
                        n="1324" xml:id="pg1324"/> with Brit Con assuming interests ur district upon
                    closure to ensure no Amcits’ property there left unprotected by reason
                    differences consular districts.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1689-f1" n="65" xml:id="d1689-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram No. 89, September 28, 6 p. m., to the Vice Consul at Chungking;
                    paragraph A referred to British agreement to afford protection to American
                    citizens in China upon closure of U.S. diplomatic and consular offices and named
                    specific services which the Department proposed to ask the British to perform
                    for U.S. citizens. These services included financial assistance, welfare
                    whereabouts, assistance in case of physical danger or distress, reports of
                    death, conservation of personal property, reports of births and notarial
                    service. No visa or passport services would be required.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
